WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:04.140
I'm changing my tie to an alkali metal tie. 

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:07.900
You have an alkali metal tie? It's a new tie I just got.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:13.780
This one's a Dutch one. I was given it on Saturday 

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:22.400
So here you can see we have the alkali metals (And where are they?) Here.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.870
And the alkali metals are very reactive. 

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:29.890
So, we've seen that lithium doesn't react very much 

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.420
but then as you go to sodium 

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:37.650
and then to potassium 

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.370
and rubidium 

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.720
the violence of the reaction gets more and more and more.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.990
In cesium it's even greater. 

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:54.450
I don't know wether the people making this tie were so frightened that they didn't put it on.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:01:00.170
But in each of these cases the reaction is removing one electron after another,

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:04.240
one electron from the outer shell of the atom,

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.500
and the bigger the atom, the easier it is to remove the electron.

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.880
And as you go down the periodic table, 

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.870
the size of the atom increases so it's easier to remove the electron.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:21.850
So francium would almost certainly be even bigger than cesium,

00:01:22.290 --> 00:01:24.130
because it's a whole row lower down. 

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.280
And so, that should react with water with enormous violence.

00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:33.220
But, unfortunately, francium is very radioactive 

00:01:34.350 --> 00:01:39.240
and you can't get a nice lump of francium the sort of size that you'd like to throw in water

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.720
to make the YouTube video of the century. 

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:50.610
Francium was named after France, was discovered by one of Madame Curie's pupils

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.390
in 1939, five years after Madame Curie herself died.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:59.100
I always thought it was Madame Curie herself that discovered it

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.530
but in fact it was discovered after her death. 

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:06.100
But its properties would probably be  similar to those of cesium

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.400
if it was not very radioactive. 

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:12.160
But radioactivity like that makes it very difficult for chemists

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:15.720
to study the chemistry in enormous detail. 

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:18.930
Chemical reactions are really quite sensitive to temperature

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.360
and radioactive materials, as they decay, give out energy

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.290
so the compounds themselves tend to heat up. 

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.800
A lump of Plutonium, which is very radioactive, is really quite warm to touch.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:37.680
And francium, which I think is more radioactive, is probably very warm.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.540
So I think the melting point of francium will be lower than that of cesium

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:45.730
because the melting points go down the group, 

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.700
and because it will be generating heat, 

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:52.420
almost certainly francium will be liquid at room temperature.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:54.950
But I've never seen it.  

00:02:55.600 --> 00:02:57.830
It might well be colored, as well, because cesium is golden colored

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.330
so I would not be surprised if francium was red. 

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.280
But I have no idea. And I'm not sure if anybody knows.

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:06.780
. . . . . . . . . . . .
